    "blurb": "The show delivers a smoking Easy Wind and an intense Loser, while the second set shines with a fiery Hard To Handle solo and a soaring Goin' Down The Road. Jerry’s guitar is crisp and Phil’s bass anchors the mellow vibe; soundboard recordings capture the performance in excellent detail.",
    "review": "The Manhattan Center hosts a memorable performance from April 4‑1971 that captures the band at a pivotal moment in their early‑’70 evolution. From the opening of \"Bertha\" through a relaxed first set, the Dead slip into a smoking \"Easy Wind\" and an intense \"Loser\" that showcase Jerry’s crystal‑clear lead tone and Phil’s hollow‑bass warmth. The second set ramps up the excitement with Bob delivering a jazzy solo on \"Hard To Handle,\" a soaring rendition of \"Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad,\" and a heartfelt take on \"Morning Dew.\" Throughout, Pigpen adds his signature organ swells while Bill Kreutzmann locks in a solid groove that propels the group forward. With several superb soundboard captures available, listeners can hear every nuance of this high‑energy April ’71 set – a true snapshot of the Dead’s magical chemistry at that time.",
